This file describes bugs that are not yet fixed and features that are not
yet implemented. Rewritten 2026-10-17 after the v1.1.33 release from a
full read-through of lib/, cli/, daemon/, hub/, snmpd/, the Makefile, CI
and the man pages. Every item cites file:line as of 1.1.33 (ae2af46).
Confidence is "confirmed" (full code path read) unless marked
"plausible" (depends on an OS/library fact not verifiable in-repo).
Items from the previous TODO that are still open are carried over in
section 10; everything previously marked [FIXED] is gone from here and
lives in CHANGELOG.md. Cleaned again 2026-10-30 at 1.1.69: the [FIXED in
1.1.34 … 1.1.69] entries that had piled up since were removed (each is in
its CHANGELOG.md section), and what they still left open is listed below
as its own item.

Priority legend: HIGH = wrong or dangerous behaviour reachable in a
normal deployment; MED = incorrect result, data/resource leak or a
documented feature that does not work; LOW = hygiene, edge cases, dead
code, doc drift.

The bhyve/VM track is in TODO2.

=== 1. Blocker: the rootless (1.0.0) model is not actually reachable ===

* (HIGH) exec_in_jail follow-ups:
    - no on-hardware run yet: the whole rootless `crate run` path
      (ensure_user_root → create_jail → mounts → exec_in_jail …) has
      never been exercised end to end; docs/on-hardware-validation.md
      §4.1 is the first thing to run.
    - NAT mode under rootless (acquire_fw_lease / release_fw_lease,
      1.1.57) and rootless bridge mode on a host with forwarding=0 are
      not validated on hardware either (on-hardware-validation §4).

* (HIGH) remaining direct privileged calls under rootless:
    - Util::ensureKernelModuleIsLoaded (ipfw_nat, if_bridge, ng_*)
      now checks kldfind first and, unprivileged, tells the operator
      which module to load instead of failing in kldload — loading
      modules stays a host-admin step by design.
    - lib/inter_dns.cpp writes /etc/hosts and the unbound conf.d file
      directly (root-only by nature). 1.1.56: refuses up front as
      non-root with the reason; moving the rebuild into crated as a
      verb is still open.
    - stack.cpp (bridge create, unbound on :53, /etc/hosts injection
      into jails) — stack is root-only today. 1.1.56: `stack up`/`down`
      refuse up front as non-root with the reason instead of failing
      half-way with containers already started.
    - `logs` is overridable from ~/.config/crate/crate.yml, so a user
      still chooses where their own audit trail goes (the daemon's
      audit.log is the record that counts for privops).
    - `create_bridge` only accepts cloner names (bridge<N>); a spec
      with auto_create_bridge and a renamed bridge (e.g. "lan0") gets a
      clear 400 under rootless — needs create + `name` rename in the
      daemon if that is wanted.

=== 2. crated privops / HTTP — security ===

* (MED, residual of the 1.1.35 canonicalisation) the realpath() check
  and the nmount()/jail -c/devfs call are two steps; a symlink swapped
  in between still wins the race. Closing it needs fd-based operation:
  open(O_RESOLVE_BENEATH) relative to the caller's prefix and
  nmount with "fspath" replaced by an fd ("fd" iov) / jail path via
  fdescfs, or at least a second realpath() after the operation with
  rollback. Same for lib/run_gui.cpp's mount targets (lstat then
  mount). Low practical exposure (needs a compromised jail racing
  its own operator's `crate run`), but the only remaining gap in the
  path chain.

* (by design, documented) a pool-scoped ADMIN token can POST
  /imports/:name + /containers/:name/start for a name in its pool and
  have crated run that .crate as root (the spec may declare host
  shares/scripts). trust-model §2b already states admin tokens are
  host-wide, not pool-confined; only viewer/pool-scoped tokens carry
  isolation. Making this real needs the spawned `crate run` to drop to
  the token's mapped uid — part of the rootless §1 work, not a gate.

* (MED) Synchronous HTTP handlers remain synchronous — by choice in
  1.1.37 (the worker pool now absorbs them): routes.cpp stop (≤ 11 s),
  restart (stop phase ≤ 11 s, then spawn), export (`tar | xz --extreme`
  inline, minutes). `crate migrate` reads the export response (file,
  size, sha256) and then downloads, so making export asynchronous
  (202 + poll) needs a migrate client change in the same release;
  stop/restart could return 202 after SIGTERM with a detached
  wait/KILL child, but that changes the documented `stopped:true`
  contract. Decide API shape first, then do both ends.

* (LOW) Registry write happens after `jail -c` returns (:663-671);
  until then the new jid/path are unowned (bootstrap Allow).
* (LOW) privops_socket_mode gets no tightness warning unlike
  listen.unix_mode.
* (LOW) handlers at privops_handlers.cpp :171,560,577 return raw
  strerror/e.what() that may echo host paths. (jsonError escaping
  fixed in 1.1.35.)

=== 3. Networking runtime ===

* (MED, left open by 1.1.38) IPv4 forwarding is restored only when
  allowOutbound(): run.cpp enables net.inet.ip.forwarding for every jail
  with `net:` when it was 0 (root path), run_net.cpp restores it only in
  the outbound teardown, so inbound-only jails leave it at 1. Rootless
  goes through release_fw_lease (1.1.57). Checked at 1.1.69.
* (LOW) ipfw_ops.cpp:85 treats EINVAL from IP_FW_XGET as "native API
  available" — EINVAL can also mean an unsupported opcode on an older
  kernel; needs a FreeBSD check before changing.
* (LOW) mac_ops.cpp removeUgidfwRules deletes by list-line index (a
  concurrent add shifts indexes); the native path should match rules
  by content.
* (LOW) healthcheck `retries: 0` means the first failure is UNHEALTHY —
  that is the documented meaning of zero retries; left as is.

=== 4. crate stack ===

* (MED) allocateIpPool is in-process only — not persisted, not
  reconciled with network_lease; two `stack up` runs of different
  stacks on one subnet can hand out the same address. Needs the lease
  file (lib/ip_alloc) or a stack-level lease under /var/run/crate.
* (LOW) no unit test round-trips every Spec field through mergeSpecs:
  lib/spec.cpp is not in TEST_LINK_SRCS (yaml-cpp), so the test needs
  either a pure "field list" or a FreeBSD functional test.
* (LOW) network_policy: a rule whose `proto` is omitted produces an
  ipfw syntax error at addRule (now cleaned up, but still fails the
  stack); validate rule.proto at parse.

=== 5. Spec parsing, config, registries ===

* (LOW) firewall/allow-tcp|udp with a map value and mac_portacl
  non-map / allow_ports non-sequence are still silently ignored
  (spec.cpp firewall / security_advanced blocks) — mirror the
  files/ handling.
* (LOW) Duplicate top-level keys: only `scripts` is checked
  (spec.cpp); other sections are processed twice (lists append) or
  last-wins. (plausible — yaml-cpp iteration semantics)
* (LOW) locs.cpp:18-22 static initialiser calls getSysctlString; a
  throw there is std::terminate before main.
* (LOW) `unless-stopped` == `always` in cli/main.cpp; no backoff
  between restarts.

=== 6. crate doctor ===

* (LOW) doctor's auto-fw anchor probe (checkAutoFwRules) does not know
  the running jail's `_pid<N>` xname and probes `crate/<name>_pid?`
  literally — make it list `pfctl -a crate -s Anchors` and match the
  prefix.
* (LOW) `crate doctor --json` has no schema version field; consumers
  cannot tell 1.1.x output shapes apart.

=== 7. Lifecycle, storage, GUI, monitoring, DNS, VPN ===

* (MED, left open by 1.1.69) crated's control-socket start/restart
  cannot start a --warm-base container (404): SpecRegistry::lookup
  returns "" for the `<name> warm-base=<dataset>` entry form.
* (LOW) lifecycle.cpp: (the `crate logs ../../x` traversal is fixed
  since 1.1.48, WarmPure::validateJailName) JSON at :180-189 emits jail->name / rctl values
  unescaped (non-crate jails can have arbitrary names). Follow mode
  never notices rotation/truncation.
* (LOW) throttle.cpp:98-141 `--clear --show` clears, then errors
  "at least one of --ingress..."; retune.cpp:41-42 `--clear KEY`
  alone is rejected; retune_pure.cpp:35 lists `cpuset`, not an RCTL
  resource; isHumanSize accepts 1.5M which rctl rejects;
  parseHumanSize (:127) can overflow long and is unused.
* (LOW) clean.cpp deletes orphan throttle RULES (20000+) but never
  the dummynet PIPES (kPipeBase 10000+; needs `ipfw pipe list`
  parsing). clean.cpp:97-98 iface-record liveness uses
  path.find(name) substring match. (comment fixed in 1.1.48)
* (LOW) import.cpp:262,272 outFile == archivePath is a raw string
  compare (`-o foo.crate` on ./foo.crate passes, copy_file fails);
  ImportPure::archiveHasTraversal (import_pure.cpp:21) rejects any
  entry containing `..` (e.g. foo..bar); import re-runs the full
  decrypt+decompress pipeline 3x.
* (LOW) export.cpp:52 prefix match jail-<target>- makes `web` resolve
  jail-web-2-xxxx; first hit wins. snmpd/collector.cpp:66 and
  stack.cpp (section 4) have the same substring/prefix bug.
* (LOW) zfs_ops.cpp:362 ::write return ignored; mount.cpp:61 strdup
  NULL unchecked; create.cpp:50-59 guessCrateName derefs
  *runServices.rbegin() when both cmd and services are empty
  (plausible — depends on validate ordering); create.cpp:564 chowns
  output to getuid() (under sudo that is root).
* (LOW) autoname_pure.cpp:16,24 std::gmtime (non-reentrant) — safe in
  the CLI, unsafe if ever called from the threaded daemon.
* (LOW) jail_query.cpp:141 fallback parses `jls -N`, whose first
  column is the NAME → jid always 0, rest misparsed; breaks on paths
  with spaces. A command legitimately exiting 126 is mistaken for
  jail_attach failure and re-executed via jexec (double execution).
  (setuid/setgid return now checked — 1.1.48.)
* (LOW) vnc_server.cpp:67 XGetImage with start-time dimensions; after
  `gui resize` (gui.cpp:402) BadMatch hits Xlib's default handler
  which exit()s the whole `crate run` (no XSetErrorHandler) →
  RunAtEnd teardown skipped. (plausible)
* (LOW) run_gui.cpp:1187-1194 copyX11Auth still copies host
  .Xauthority/.ICEauthority for gui.mode compositor/none/wayland;
  :878,889 setupClipboard reads only x11Options, ignoring gui.mode
  nested; :227-228 fork failure leaves the vncpw file behind; :1084
  `vnc: true` with backend drm silently ignored; wayvnc's 127.0.0.1 is
  the jail's loopback in a vnet jail so the advertised port (:1157) is
  unreachable from the host.
* (LOW) inspect.cpp:170 started_at always 0; :57-68 ip6/mac never
  populated; inspect_pure.cpp:154-159 mount parsing breaks on paths
  containing " on " or " (".
* (LOW) capsicum_ops.cpp enterCapabilityMode / initCapDns /
  resolveDns / limitFdRights have no callers (only
  initCapSyslog/logSyslog via audit.cpp); initCapDns leaks g_capdns
  on repeat calls; unused WARN macro. drm_session.cpp:18,53,63 single
  global g_deviceId — a second openDevice orphans the first.

=== 8. crate-hub ===

* (LOW) hub/store.cpp shares one sqlite3 handle between the poller
  thread and the HTTP threads with no mutex and no busy_timeout; it
  works because sqlite's default serialized threading mode locks per
  call. Add a Store mutex (or one connection per thread) before any
  multi-statement operation is added.

* (MED) Dashboard actions still go browser → crated directly with
  https:// hard-coded, and crated emits no CORS headers, so the
  cross-origin fetch is blocked. Route start/stop/restart/destroy
  through the hub (POST /api/v1/nodes/:node/containers/:name/<verb>
  with the hub holding per-node admin tokens — a trust-model change)
  or document the dashboard as read-only.

* (LOW) store.cpp: one sqlite3* shared by poller and HTTP threads
  with no mutex (safe only with SQLITE_THREADSAFE=1 — plausible).
  poller.cpp: a down node costs two 5 s timeouts so N down nodes can
  exceed poll_interval. api.cpp /api/v1/ha/orders emits failover
  orders from the hub's own view only (hub-side partition → orders
  while the primary is healthy; mitigated because `crate migrate`
  needs the source).

=== 9. crate-snmpd ===

* (MED) CRATE-MIB table and traps are not implemented but documented
  as the product: mib.cpp answers noSuchInstance for every non-scalar
  OID; resolveGetNext walks only 4 scalars; sendTrap only logs.
  snmpd/CRATE-MIB.txt:65-165 defines an 11-column table plus
  NOTIFICATION-TYPEs; README.md:761-763 says the agent "exposes
  container metrics". collector.h:20 says "CPU% x 100" but
  collector.cpp:52 stores raw percent; netRx/netTx/uptimeTicks never
  populated. Either implement the table + Notify PDUs or trim the MIB
  and README to the four scalars.

=== 10. Carried over from the previous TODO ===

* (LOW, left open by 1.1.51) validate the run.cpp exception-path
  teardown order (network / lease / firewall RunAtEnd declared before
  destroyJail) on a real FreeBSD host (docs/on-hardware-validation.md).
* (LOW, left open by 1.1.51) run_net.cpp createBridgeEpair / the extras
  loop still leak one epairN pair when the *second* half of a single
  attempt fails — FreeBSD-only to verify.

* (LOW batch, still open) daemon/routes.cpp:1014 import tmp file
  <name>.crate.tmp.<pid> is constant across threads (two concurrent
  imports of one name interleave; no fsync before rename); capture
  loops treat read()==-1 as EOF (EINTR); ws_console.cpp select() with
  fds >= FD_SETSIZE.

* (security, HIGH-if-reachable, latent) libvirt VM track
  (lib/vm_spec.cpp:97-184 generateDomainXml embeds name / disk /
  bridge / hostPath / tag unescaped into domain XML handed to
  virDomainCreateXML as root; vm_run.cpp:250-388 and vm_stack.cpp:88
  build root-written paths from vmName / vol.tag with no traversal or
  newline check; memoryToKiB val*1024*1024 overflow). No caller
  today (built only WITH_LIBVIRT). Decision 2026-10: kept as roadmap.
  XML-escape / validate every field BEFORE wiring it up.

=== 11. Build, CI, packaging ===

* (MED) port/Makefile `do-test` runs `make test`, which needs kyua and
  the ATF libraries (devel/atf) — add them as TEST_DEPENDS or drop the
  target; `pkg-plist` has not been verified against a real `make
  stage` + `make check-plist` run since the HUB option was added.
* (LOW) freebsd-build.yml (full) is manual-only and has not run since
  2026-06; the functional suite (tests/functional) therefore gates
  nothing. A self-hosted FreeBSD runner would make it a nightly.
* (LOW) the Linux unit workflow builds the tests but never links crate,
  crated or crate-hub; crate-hub links on Linux (yaml-cpp, sqlite3,
  openssl, cpp-httplib all in apt) and could be built there as a
  second compile gate.

=== 12. Documentation debt ===

* crated.8 AUTHENTICATION still says read endpoints are open to "any
  viewer or admin token holder" — true since 1.1.36, but the CAVEATS
  wording that unix-socket peers are all admin should point at the
  1.1.4x per-user control sockets as the alternative.
* crate.5 has no entry for `network: auto` at the top level of a spec
  (spec.cpp:523) — confirm what it does today (auto bridge + pool
  lease?) before documenting it.
* docs/on-hardware-validation.md needs a runbook entry for each
  "validate on a real FreeBSD host" note in sections 3, 4, 10.

=== 13. Test gaps (unit, pure modules) ===

* (MED) the tests link only the *_pure objects, so everything that
  lives in lib/spec.cpp (yaml-cpp) is untested at the parser level:
  children_max, cpuset, disk_quota, the `x11:` map vs scalar, the
  extended-options unknown-key rejection, healthcheck/restart maps,
  mergeSpecs round-trip of every field. Either link lib/spec.cpp +
  yaml-cpp into a dedicated spec parser test binary (Linux CI would
  need libyaml-cpp-dev) or move the key-level validation into
  SpecPure functions that take already-extracted scalars.
* (LOW) routes.cpp's "which endpoints need which role" table is
  inline in the handlers; lifting it into RoutesPure (method, path →
  min role) would make the viewer-token rule testable.
* (LOW) console.cpp builds the `login -f <user>` argv inline; a pure
  builder (ConsolePure::loginArgv) would pin the 1.1.x split fix.
* (LOW) the ws-console pin tests AuthPure, not the call site in
  daemon/ws_console.cpp — that needs the daemon compiled into a test.
* Not unit-testable, needs the on-hardware runbook: everything in
  section 1, pf anchor lifecycle (3.), lease/registry lock race,
  stack up/down, privops symlink canonicalisation.

=== 14. Medium priority — operational quality (features) ===

* (feature) Full base-system update inside a running jail.
  `crate update --pkg-only` (0.8.41; broken today, see 7.) covers
  packages only; the base system is in use by the jail's processes,
  so an in-place freebsd-update is not safe. Needs snapshot → apply
  into a clone (or with the jail stopped) → verify → promote or roll
  back. Interacts with the warm-template cache (0.7.5) and the restart
  policy. Design pass needed first.

=== 15. Out of scope (separate repository) ===

* Admin/dashboard UI for the datacenter view. The hub already exposes
  /api/v1/{nodes,datacenters,aggregate,containers} and the per-node
  crated daemon owns the F2 mutating surface. A richer admin SPA will
  live in its own repository; the minimal vanilla-JS dashboard in
  hub/web/ stays as a built-in fallback (after 8. is fixed it will
  actually show data).

=== 16. Low priority — future enhancements (architectural) ===

* (feature) Layered images — ZFS-based layering through clones
  instead of monolithic .crate archives. Touches create/run/import/
  export simultaneously; the archive format becomes a manifest of
  parent snapshots. Blocked on operator input: canonical layer
  sources, goal (disk vs cold-start vs hub bandwidth), interaction
  with the ZFS-send backup/replicate path and per-user ZFS prefixes.

* (security) True getpeereid(2)-based unix-socket auth for the main
  HTTP API in crated. docs/getpeereid-main-api-refactor.md argues for
  deferring: the per-tenant gate lives on control sockets (Plane 2)
  and the privops socket. The big refactor waits for a concrete
  per-tenant feature on the main HTTP API. Operators can scope
  crated.sock via listen.unix_owner / unix_group / unix_mode (0.8.19).

* Sections 14 and 16 (features) need operator input before any code:
  base-system update design (snapshot → clone → verify → promote),
  layered images (canonical layer sources, goal). Not started.

=== 17. Ideas borrowed from bwrap / firejail (Linux sandboxes) ===

Context (2026-10-22 review). bubblewrap is a bare primitive —
namespaces + bind mounts + a seccomp fd, no profiles, no state,
unprivileged via user namespaces (Flatpak's engine). firejail is a
setuid wrapper with ~1000 per-application profiles, seccomp/caps,
`--private-*`, netfilter, an X11 sandbox, a D-Bus filter and runtime
commands. Both share the HOST rootfs through namespaces; crate runs a
full jail (own userland, VNET, ZFS). Most of their isolation knobs
already have a crate equivalent: dirs/files share ≈ --bind, x11/mode
nested ≈ --x11=xephyr, dbus/policy ≈ --dbus-user.filter, limits ≈
--rlimit-*, capsicum / mac_bsdextended / mac_portacl ≈ seccomp + caps,
dns_filter ≈ --dns, terminal/devfs_ruleset ≈ --private-dev;
clipboard/mode has no firejail counterpart. ZFS snapshots/COW, stacks
with their own DNS, migrate/backup/replicate, VNC and the hub exist in
neither — worth a comparison table in README. NOT worth porting:
seccomp profiles one-to-one (Capsicum + MAC cover it), AppImage,
Landlock, --restrict-namespaces.

* DONE 1.1.58 — thin jail on nullfs, `base/mode: host-ro`
  (lib/thin_jail_pure, +CRATE.THIN manifest). Decisions taken: the
  writable layer (/etc /var /root /tmp) is extracted from the cached
  base.txz of the host release (pristine /etc, no host secrets copied)
  rather than generated from a template; /usr/local stays per jail
  and writable (pkg), so /usr is mounted as its subdirectories, not
  whole; the default directory list is frozen into the manifest and
  `base/host-dirs` overrides it; release mismatch is a warning, not a
  refusal (a host upgrade upgrading every thin jail is the point).
  Left for later, on hardware: a `tmpfs:` option for /tmp of thin
  jails (item below), `crate info`/`inspect` showing the bootstrap
  mode, and §14's full base update becomes "re-create the crate" for
  this class of container.

* DONE 1.1.59 — per-application profiles, `crate run <name>`
  (lib/profile_pure, lib/profile). Decisions taken: no `profiles/`
  directory in the tree and no second copy in the plist — the
  installed examples ARE the shipped profile set, searched after
  ~/.config/crate/profiles/ and /usr/local/share/crate/profiles/
  (site-local, operator-created); the .crate is built on first use
  into ~/.local/share/crate/profiles/ and rebuilt on mtime; the
  `.local` merge is YAML-level (maps deep, lists appended, `options`
  by option name, scalars replaced) because +CRATE.SPEC is a copy of
  the spec FILE, so a Spec-level merge never reaches `crate run`; a
  .local may not set security_advanced, dbus, base, scripts,
  encrypted, securelevel, socket_proxy, nor share outside $HOME.
  Profile names with '+' (chromium+tor and friends) are not valid
  jail names — copy them under a dash name. Leftovers: `crate
  profile list|show|path`, a `--rebuild` flag, `.local` under
  /usr/local/etc for site-wide tweaks.

* DONE 1.1.68 — `crate stack up` composes the member spec at the YAML
  level (lib/stack_spec on top of Profile::mergeYaml) and builds from
  that file, so the template, the pool address, the hosts/DNS scripts
  and the named volumes are what +CRATE.SPEC holds and what the
  detached `crate run -f` executes. Decisions taken: YAML-level rather
  than serialising the Spec — there is no Spec→YAML emitter and one
  would have to track every field forever; `${VAR}` placeholders stay
  in the archive and `crate run` now substitutes its --var values when
  it parses +CRATE.SPEC (it never did; the stack passes every member's
  vars that way), so a `from_file` secret is not written into the
  .crate; a scripts section in scalar/list form is rewritten to the
  map form under the parser's own names ("" / script#N) instead of
  being replaced; `template:` is resolved like `--template` (name in
  the template dirs, path relative to the stack file). Found on the
  way: the pool address never reached a member, so a stack with
  `ip_range` had /etc/hosts and network_policy rules naming addresses
  no jail held. Leftovers: `collectContainerIPs` still reads the
  static IP through the Spec-level mergeSpecs (read-only, same
  result); the composed file could be kept next to `<name>.crate` for
  inspection (`--keep-specs`); runbook §4k is the first real run.

* DONE 1.1.60 — `crate profile-from-trace [-o spec] -- <cmd>`
  (lib/trace_pure, lib/trace). Decisions taken: the program runs on
  the HOST under `truss -f`, unsandboxed (a wide "recording" jail is
  left for later); stat() results are collected but do not produce
  shares (only successful opens do); nothing under /usr/local is ever
  shared — executed files become pkg/install via `pkg which`, files
  that were only read a comment; outbound destinations map to the
  wan/lan/host/dns classes of options/net/outbound, not to
  firewall/allow-tcp|udp (ports differ between runs, destinations are
  kept as a comment); the YAML is rendered by hand so the module is
  yaml-free and unit-tested on Linux. The truss line shapes come from
  the 14.x sources, not from a recorded run — runbook §4c confirms
  them. Leftovers: recording-jail mode (`--in-jail`), read-only shares
  once dirs/share gains `:ro`, a sound mapping once a sound option
  exists, `--merge` into an existing spec.

* DONE 1.1.61 — `crate audit <target>` (lib/jail_audit_pure,
  lib/jail_audit). Decisions taken: the probe is ONE /bin/sh script
  with base-system tools (one exec_in_jail round trip; a missing tool
  is text in its section, not a failed run), the parsing happens on
  the host so the pipeline is unit-tested on Linux; the allow.* flags
  are read as the jail sees them (security.jail.* view sysctls), not
  from jail(8) params on the host; host sockets are recognised by
  sitting inside a nullfs mount; the two active probes are one TCP
  connect to the default gateway (port 22 — "refused" proves
  reachability as well as "open") and one `drill` lookup, both behind
  `--offline`; WARN is reserved for clear exposures (host area rw, raw
  / disk / input devices, host sockets, hostid = host's, raw sockets /
  mount / chflags allowed, enforce_statfs 0) — securelevel, sysvipc
  and socket_af are reported, not judged. Leftovers: the `<spec>`
  form (start a throw-away jail from a spec and audit it); comparing
  the findings with the jail's +CRATE.SPEC (the spec registry knows
  the crate path) to flag what the spec did not ask for; per-fs
  mount_*_allowed flags.

* DONE 1.1.62 — device classes, `devices: {sound, gpu, video, input,
  usb, serial}` (lib/devices_pure, run.cpp, privops apply_devfs_rules).
  Found on the way: crate mounted the jail's devfs from the host with
  NO ruleset, so every jail without terminal/devfs_ruleset saw the
  host's whole /dev (disks, mem/kmem, keyboards) — the 0.8.11 "auto-
  unhide /dev/dri" and 0.8.45 compositor unhide added rules to a mount
  that had no ruleset to add them to. Decisions taken: every jail now
  gets a PRIVATE ruleset (10000 + pid % 50000; delset before fill, so a
  reused number cannot leak rules) with hide-all + the basic nodes
  spelled out (not `include 1..3`: does not depend on the host having
  loaded /etc/defaults/devfs.rules) + the classes; `gl`, `video` and
  the gui gpu/auto/compositor modes imply their classes; a custom
  terminal/devfs_ruleset is `include`d instead of the base and the
  classes go on top; `devices: all` restores the old full view; a
  devfs failure is a loud yellow warning, not a refusal (unverified on
  hardware — the one place where failing closed could have taken every
  jail down); one new privops verb `apply_devfs_rules` (closed rulespec
  grammar: hide | include N | path GLOB unhide, path-scoped by
  mount_path) replaces the per-pattern add_devfs_unhide_rule calls,
  which stay for compatibility. Leftovers: `crate audit` could compare
  the devfs view with the spec's classes; a `sound` *option* (OSS +
  sndio/pulse socket plumbing) is still separate from the device class;
  per-device grants (`devices/usb: [ugen0.2]`).

* DONE 1.1.63 — `dirs/share` `:ro` (suffix or a third element),
  `tmpfs: [paths]` / `{path: size}`, `etc/keep: [names]`
  (lib/mounts_pure, Spec::Share/Tmpfs/etcKeep, privops mount_tmpfs).
  Decisions taken: the share pair is {jail, host} and is now a named
  struct — the strict validate's duplicate-destination check had read
  the pair the other way round since it was written (fixed, test
  corrected); file shares cannot be :ro (hard link / copy) and say so;
  tmpfs is mounted after the shares, owned by the jail user (0700)
  inside $HOME and by root (01777) elsewhere, refused on the
  directories the jail cannot do without; etc/keep copies at CREATE
  time (cp -RpP for directories), so a crate carries the host's
  localtime/hosts/ssl from the machine it was built on — a thin jail's
  pristine /etc is the main customer; stack volumes' :ro finally
  reaches the mount (it was parsed and dropped). Leftovers: `tmpfs`
  entries for stack members; `etc/keep` refresh at run time
  (`etc/refresh`); a `dirs/share` `:nosuid`/`:noexec`.

* DONE 1.1.64 — `run/user` (RunPure::effectiveRunUser). Decisions
  taken: the default stays the operator's mirrored account (shares
  under $HOME, the X11 authority and `crate console` all assume it), so
  "non-root by default" is a statement about the ROOT operator, whose
  mirror was root — `pw groupadd root` failed, so `crate run` as root
  had not worked since the user mirror was introduced; it now runs the
  command as the dedicated `crate` account (created at run time if
  missing, so the image is not polluted for everyone else).
  `run/user: root` is the explicit opt-in (validate warns);
  `run/user: <name>` is a jail account — a package's or one `crate
  create` adds with pw(8) in the chroot (uid picked by pw; `crate run`
  repeats the check for templates and older crates), not in wheel.
  The service-only /run.sh is root-owned 0555 when the run user is not
  the mirror. Leftovers: `--nonewprivs` has no FreeBSD equivalent short
  of removing setuid binaries from the image (a `base/remove` preset
  `setuid` would do); `crate console` default user when run/user is
  set; `run/user` for stack members.

* DONE 1.1.65 — Machine-readable lifecycle (StatusPure / StatusFd).
  Decisions taken: one flat JSON object per line (strings, integers,
  booleans, epoch `ts`), so the reader is forty lines and not a JSON
  library; the stream wraps the whole run — `started` before the
  extract, `failed`+`teardown_done` after every RunAtEnd has run — so
  a consumer has one terminal rule (exited / failed / EOF).
  `exec_started` carries user+command, not a pid: execInJail is
  synchronous and the in-jail pid is not known to the supervisor.
  `net_ready` has `ip` for static/pool addresses only (DHCP is not
  known at that point). The descriptor is CLOEXEC and the write
  blocks SIGPIPE: a vanished reader must never take the jail down.
  `stack up` and crated spawn over a pipe on fd 3 and stop at
  `jail_created`; crated falls back to the jls poll only when the pipe
  could not be made. `run/timeout` is timeout(1) `-k 10` in front of
  the command (exit 124 → "timed out"), no second supervisor thread.
  Leftovers: the hub/crated REST surface does not relay the events yet
  (`GET /containers/:name/events` as SSE would be the natural home);
  `run/timeout` for stack members; a `--status-fd` for `crate create`
  (extract/pkg phases); `exec_started` pid via a pidfile the in-jail
  `sh -c` writes.

* DONE 1.1.66 — Desktop integration (WrapPure, lib/wrap.cpp).
  Decisions taken: per-user files only (~/.local/bin and the XDG
  applications dir), never /usr/local/bin symlinks the way firecfg does
  — crate is not setuid and a system-wide shadow of `firefox` would
  surprise every other user; the launcher is a script, not a symlink,
  so `crate run <profile>` is spelled out and the marker travels with
  the file. The marker is what `remove`/`list` trust: a file without it
  is the operator's and is neither replaced (without --force) nor
  deleted. Terminal= is derived from the profile's x11/gui. Leftovers:
  `wrap install --all` over the shipped examples; a `wrap check` that
  reports launchers whose profile vanished; MIME types from the spec
  (`MimeType=` so file associations pick the container); an uninstall
  hook when a profile is removed.

* DONE 1.1.67 — `privacy/hostid`, `privacy/hostname` (PrivacyPure).
  Decisions taken: the kernel is the source of truth — the jail is
  created with `host.hostuuid`/`host.hostid` (jail_setv; whitelisted in
  the privops create_jail parameters), and /etc/hostid inside is written
  to match, rather than only writing the file (rc.d/hostid does not run
  in a crate jail, so a file alone changes nothing). The hostid is
  derived from the UUID exactly as rc.d/hostid does (first 32 bits of
  md5), with a self-contained MD5 so the arithmetic is unit-tested on
  Linux. The TODO's premise that the hostname was already per container
  was wrong: crate passed the HOST's name as host.hostname, hence
  `privacy/hostname` too (`random` = crate-<8 hex>). Default stays
  `host` for both — identical jail to before. Leftovers: a per-run
  machine-id for programs that read /etc/machine-id or D-Bus's
  machine-id (`dbus-uuidgen` in the jail at start); `privacy/hostname`
  with a domain (`host.domainname`); a `privacy` row in `crate inspect`.

